  "schedule": "Day 1 Upper Power, Day 2 Lower Power, Day 3 Rest, Day 4 Back and Shoulders Hypertrophy, Day 5 Lower Hypertrophy, Day 6 Chest and Arms Hypertrophy, Day 7 Rest. Session length is not stated by the author; 90 min is an estimate.",
  "progression": {
    "type": "CUSTOM",
    "description": "Author's version on Biolayne (posted 5.30.2016) uses the same template as the 2011 SimplyShredded original. Power days: 3-5 reps for 3-5 working sets on one main compound each for lower body, pressing and pulling, resting until fully recovered (5-6 min if needed). Speed work: 6-8 sets of 3 (template shows 6) at 65-70% of your normal 3-5RM on the same power movement used earlier in the week, moved explosively, max 90 s rest; reduce the load if using bands or chains. Hypertrophy work: 8-20 reps, 1-2 min rest, about 50-75% more volume than the power days. Stop 1-2 reps short of failure on most sets; once adapted, take only the last 1-2 sets of each exercise to failure. Avoid failure entirely for the first 2-4 weeks (the FAQ says 3-6 weeks), and do not train to failure consistently for more than 6 weeks in a row. Rotate power movements every 2-3 weeks. Beginners to this volume may cut one assistance/auxiliary exercise per day. The article gives no numeric weight-increase rule, so incrementKg is null.",
    "incrementKg": {
      "upper": null,
      "lower": null
    },
    "deload": "Deload once every 6-12 weeks, or as needed (FAQ: not scheduled, taken when needed), for 1-3 weeks: same routine at 60-70% of normal weights. On a plateau, first change power/accessory exercises, then attack sticking points: deficit deadlifts (2-4 in box) or rack pulls from just below the knee, paused below-parallel box squats, good mornings or deep hack squats, triceps work (close-grip presses, dips, skull crushers) or 3-count paused presses; deload if that fails."
  },
